\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 7: Models of Censored Dependent Variable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract

\series bold
\size normal
Censored and Truncated Model: 
\series default
Specification and interpretation of the model; Estimation (MLE and EM);
 Type 1 Tobit Model; Type II Tobit Model (Incidental Truncation)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Standard (singularly censored) Tobit Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the latent variable model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}^{*}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t},\ u_{t}\sim\mbox{i.i.d.}(0,\sigma^{2})
\]

\end_inset

where observations on regressors 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 are fully available, but observations on the dependent variable are censored:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}\begin{cases}
y_{t}^{*} & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}>0\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

This is called the standard (singularly censored) 
\emph on
Tobit
\emph default
 model when 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 has a normal distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Tobin's (1958) model is a regression equation of the ratio of total durable
 goods expenditure to disposable income on the age of the head of the household
 and the ratio of liquid assets to disposable income.
 Another example of early application is Fair's (1978) model of the number
 of extra-marital affairs on sex, age, number of years married, number of
 children, education, occupation, degree of religiousness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Properties of the OLSE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that the error term 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Gauss-Markove assumptions to that the OLS is BLUE if observations
 on the dependent variable are not censored.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{+}\equiv y_{t}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 denote the positive observed data.
 One may apply the OLS to estimate the Tobit model using
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
positively observed data only, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{+}=X_{t}\beta+\eta_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
all observations (including zeros), 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=X_{t}\beta+\epsilon_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is biased and inconsistent in both cases.
 To show this, consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
E(y_{t}^{+})\equiv E(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t}^{*}>0)=E(X_{t}\beta+u_{t}|u_{t}<-X_{t}\beta)=X_{t}\beta+E(u_{t}|u_{t}<-X_{t}\beta)=X_{t}\beta+E(u_{t}^{+})\neq X_{t}\beta\\
E(y_{t})=P(y_{t}>0)E(y_{t}|y_{t}>0)+P(y_{t}=0)E(y_{t}|y_{t}=0)=\left[1-F(-X_{t}\beta)\right]E(y_{t}^{+})\equiv P_{t}^{+}E(y_{t}^{+})\neq X_{t}\beta
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is the cumulative distribution function of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}^{+})=E(u_{t}|u_{t}>-X_{t}\beta)$
\end_inset

 is the truncated mean of the error term, and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{+}=1-F(-X_{t}\beta)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of being above the limit.
 This implies that error terms in the regression equations in (a) and (b)
 above do not have zero means, and heace OLSE is biased.
 Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(y_{t}) & = & \int y_{t}f(y_{t})dy_{t}\\
 & = & \int P(y_{t}>0)y_{t}f(y_{t}|y_{t}>0)+P(y_{t}=0)(0)f(y_{t}|y_{t}=0)dy_{t}\\
 & = & P(y_{t}>0)\int y_{t}f(y_{t}|y_{t}>0)dy_{t}\\
 & = & P(y_{t}>0)E(y_{t}|y_{t}>0)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{t})$
\end_inset

 is the unconditional pdf.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
Computation of the truncated mean 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t}^{+})=E(u_{t}|u_{t}>-X_{t}\beta)$
\end_inset

 uses the truncated density function of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

, which is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(u_{t}|u_{t}>c)=\begin{cases}
\frac{f(u_{t})}{1-F(c)} & \mbox{if}\ u_{t}>c\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c=-X_{t}\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is the probability density function of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The mean of this distribution is computed by the usual formula
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{t}|u_{t}>c)={\displaystyle \intop_{c}^{\infty}}z\frac{f(z)}{1-F(c)}\ dz
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Marginal Effects of Regressors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can consider several effects of a regressor: the marginal effect on the
 means of the latent dependent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}^{*})$
\end_inset

, of the censored dependent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t})$
\end_inset

, of the positive dependent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}^{+})$
\end_inset

, of the negative dependent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}^{-})$
\end_inset

, and the probability of being above the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{+}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial E(y_{t}^{*})}{\partial X_{tk}} & = & \beta_{k}\\
\frac{\partial E(y_{t}^{+})}{\partial X_{tk}} & = & \beta_{k}+\frac{\partial E(u_{t}^{+})}{\partial X_{tk}}\\
\frac{\partial P_{t}^{+}}{\partial X_{tk}} & = & f(-X_{t}\beta)\beta_{k}\\
\frac{\partial E(y_{t})}{\partial X_{tk}} & = & P_{t}^{+}\frac{\partial E(y_{t}^{+})}{\partial X_{tk}}+E(y_{t}^{+})\frac{\partial P_{t}^{+}}{\partial X_{tk}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The last expression shows the decomposition of the marginal effect of a
 regressor on the expected censored dependent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 into two components.
 This decomposition is suggest in Tobin (1958) and worked out in detail
 by McDonald and Moffitt (1980).
 In the example of household expenditure on consumer durables, the first
 term captures the effect of the regressor on the expenditures of those
 who had positive expenditures.
 Tobin called the first term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the size of non-limit responses.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The second term caputres the effect of the regressor on the change of the
 households which have a positive expenditure, either moving from positive
 expenditure to zero expenditure, or vice versa.
 If a houshold moves from ther zero to positive expenditure group, its expected
 expenditure is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}^{+})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The marginal effects of a regressor are not constant except for the effect
 of the mean of the latent variable.
 They are functions of the values of all regressors.
 Therefore, the marginal effects can be evaluated at various values of the
 regressors, and it is a common practice to evaluate them at the sample
 means of continuous regressors.
 But, they can also be computed for a set of specific values of the regressors.
 When a regressors is a dscirete variable (like a 0-1 dummy variable), its
 marginal effect can be evaluated by computing the mean function at value
 0 and then at value 1.
 The difference between the two values is then take as the marginal effect
 of a binary regressor.
 This value of course depends on the choice of the values of other regressors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Estimation of Censored Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Maximum Likelihood Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Observations are either the event of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}\leq0$
\end_inset

, or the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Define a binary dummy variable for censoring
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{t}=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}=0\ (y_{t}^{*}\leq0)\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}>0\ (y_{t}^{*}>0)
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The single likelihood function is either a discrete probability 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-X_{t}\beta)=P(y_{t}^{*}\leq0)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=0$
\end_inset

, or the density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{t}^{*})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}>0$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{t}(y_{t})=F(-X_{t}\beta)^{1-d_{t}}f(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta)^{d_{t}}
\]

\end_inset

The likelihood function and the log likelihood function for all observations
 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & {\displaystyle \prod_{t=1}^{n}}\left[F(-X_{t}\beta)\right]^{1-d_{t}}\left[f(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta)\right]^{d_{t}}\\
\ln L & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\left((1-d_{t})\ln\left[F(-X_{t}\beta)\right]+d_{t}\ln\left[f(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta)\right]\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The MLE of the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is the root of the gradient vector
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial\ln L}{\partial\theta}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\left[(1-d_{t})\frac{f(-X_{t}\beta)}{F(-X_{t}\beta)}\frac{\partial f(-X_{t}\beta)}{d\theta}+d_{t}\frac{1}{f(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta)}\frac{\partial f(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta)}{\partial\theta}\right]=0
\]

\end_inset

and the asymptotic covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(n\Psi)^{-1}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 is the information matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Tobit Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Tobin assume a normal distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}|X_{t}\sim\mbox{i.i.n}(0,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(z)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(z)$
\end_inset

 be the cdf and pdf of a standard normal random variable, respectively.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(-X_{t}\beta)=P(y_{t}\leq0)=P(u_{t}\leq-X_{t}\beta)=P\left(\frac{u_{t}}{\sigma}\leq-\frac{X_{t}\beta}{\sigma}\right)=\Phi\left(-\frac{X_{t}\beta}{\sigma}\right)=1-\Phi\left(\frac{X_{t}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\equiv1-\Phi_{t}
\]

\end_inset

and the log likelihood function becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
l(\beta,\sigma^{2})={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\left\{ (1-d_{t})\ln(1-\Phi_{t})+d_{t}\left[-\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\ln(\sigma^{2})-\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\ln(2\pi)-\frac{\left(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Amemiya (1973) proved that this Tobit log-likelihood function is not globablly
 concave with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the Newton-Raphson, or the method of scoring, or the alternative
 iterative algortihm to solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 require their consistent initial estimators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The log-likelihod function can be written is transformed parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\frac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=\frac{1}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
l(\alpha,\tau)={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\left\{ \left(1-d_{t}\right)\ln\left(1-\Phi_{t}\right)+d_{t}\ln\tau-\left(\frac{d_{t}}{2}\right)\ln(2\pi)-d_{t}\frac{\left(\tau y_{t}-X_{t}\alpha\right)^{2}}{2}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{t}=\Phi\left(\frac{X_{t}\beta}{\sigma}\right)=\Phi(X_{t}\alpha)$
\end_inset

.
 Olsen (1978) showed that this log-likelihood funcitons is globally concave
 with respect to the transformed parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the log-likelihood function in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 can be maximized by finding the roots of its gradient vectors, starting
 from any arbitrary initial values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

.
 Estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 can then be recovered from the estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{1}{\hat{\tau}^{2}}\ \mbox{and}\ \hat{\beta}=\hat{\alpha}\cdot\hat{\sigma}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
EM Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Define a new dependent variable that replaces the censored value with its
 conditional expectation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{y}_{t}=\begin{cases}
y_{t}^{*} & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}>0\\
E(y_{t}^{-}) & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y_{t}^{-})\equiv E(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t}\leq0)=E(X_{t}\beta+u_{t}|u_{t}\leq-X_{t}\beta)=X_{t}\beta+E(u_{t}|u_{t}\leq-X_{t}\beta)=X_{t}\beta-\sigma\left[\frac{\phi_{t}}{\left(1-\Phi_{t}\right)}\right]
\]

\end_inset

The iterative EM algorithm follows the following procedure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Choose initial consistent estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

, and compute 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}^{-})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compute the new estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 by the OLS: 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\tilde{Y}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compute the new estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 by useing the new 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 from (ii) and non-censored data
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}d_{t}(y_{t}-X_{t}\hat{\beta})y_{t}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}d_{t}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Repeat the procedure until convergence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The EM algorithm can also be written in terms of parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

.
 Define
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{y}_{t}=\begin{cases}
\tau y_{t}^{*} & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}>0\\
\tau E(y_{t}^{-}) & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tau E(y_{t}^{-}) & = & X_{t}\alpha-\lambda_{t}\\
\lambda_{t} & = & \frac{\phi_{t}}{(1-\Phi_{t})}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The maximization step of the EM algortihm gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\alpha}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\tilde{y}
\]

\end_inset

and the estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 as the solution
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tau^{2}{\displaystyle \sum_{N_{2}}}\left(y_{t}^{*}\right)^{2}-\tau{\displaystyle \sum_{N_{2}}}(X_{t}\alpha)y_{t}^{*}-N_{2}=0\implies\alpha\tau^{2}-b\tau-c=0
\]

\end_inset

which is a quadratic function in 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 is positive, we take the positive root
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\tau}=\frac{b+\sqrt{b^{2}+4ac}}{2a}
\]

\end_inset

The iterative procedure starts with any initial values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau>0$
\end_inset

.
 Compute 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}\alpha-\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 to construct the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

, and estiamte 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 The esitmate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 is then computed as the positive root of the quadratic equation specified
 above.
 This process is repeated until convergence is achieved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Heckman's Two-Step Method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This method is based on a regression equation for only the non-censored
 sample
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}^{+}=X_{t}\beta+\left(\frac{\phi_{t}}{\Phi_{t}}\right)\sigma+\eta_{t},\ y_{t}^{+}=y_{t}>0
\]

\end_inset

or for the entire sample
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=\left(\Phi_{t}X_{t}\right)\beta+\phi_{t}\sigma+\eta_{t}
\]

\end_inset

Parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 can be estimated from these equations by OLS if we have observations on
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Heckman suggested to estimate them from a probit model of the entire sample,
 by setting the observed dependent 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Truncated Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is the case where observations on both dependent and independent variables
 are not available for a sub-population.
 The Tobit model becomes a truncated model if the sample of negative 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is not used in the estimation of the population parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 The least squares estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is biased and inconsistent in the case of truncated data set, i.e., using
 the observations of positive 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 only.
 Consider a regression equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}^{*}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t}^{+},\ y_{t}^{*}>0
\]

\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a normal random variable, the moments of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}^{+}$
\end_inset

 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(u_{t}^{+}|y_{t}^{*}>0) & = & \frac{\phi_{t}}{\Phi_{t}}\sigma\\
\mbox{Var}(u_{t}^{+}|y_{t}^{*}>0) & = & \sigma^{2}\left[1-\kappa_{t}(\kappa_{t}+X_{t}\alpha)\right],\ \kappa_{t}=\frac{\phi_{t}}{\Phi_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The LSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is biased and inconsistent because of the nonzero mean of the error term.
 The regression error term is also heteroskedastic.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A consistent estimator of the population parameters can be obtained by the
 MLE.
 The truncated density function of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{t}^{*}|y_{t}^{*}>0)=\begin{cases}
\frac{(\frac{1}{\sigma})\phi\left(\frac{y_{t}^{*}-X_{t}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}{\Phi\left(\frac{X_{t}\beta}{\sigma}\right)} & \mbox{if}\ y_{t}^{*}>0\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and hence, the log-likelihood function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
l=-{\displaystyle \sum_{t}}\ln\left(\Phi_{t}\right)-\frac{N_{2}}{2}\ln(2\pi)-\frac{N_{2}}{2}\ln(\sigma^{2})-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t}}(y_{t}^{*}-X_{t}\beta)^{2}
\]

\end_inset

where the summation is only over 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{2}$
\end_inset

 sample of positive 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Incidental Truncation - Type II Tobit Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In censored and truncated models, observations of the dependent variable
 is based on the magnitude of the dependent variable itself.
 What if the observation of the dependent variable depends on the magnitude
 (sign) of another variable? One of the earliest studies is Gronau's (1973)
 model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
w_{i} & = & X_{2i}\gamma+v_{i}\\
w_{i}^{r} & = & Z_{t}\gamma^{r}+v_{i}^{r}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the wage rate offered to individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the market regardless of the number of working hours and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}^{r}$
\end_inset

 is the reservation wage rate of individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 which is not an observable variable.
 An individual participates in the labor market and we can observe her market
 wage if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}-w_{i}^{r}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Heckman's (1974) paper is motivated by his observation of the research on
 the labor supply of married women, included wage rates, hours of work,
 and decision to work.
 Typical research estimated the labor supply by estimating separately an
 equation for hours of work and an equation for the probability of work,
 or estimate the same labor supply function for the working and non-working
 women assigning zero hour for non-working women.
 His model is a structural model which includes the functions of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the probability that a woman works, her hours of work, her observed wage
 rate, and her asking wage or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

shadow price of time.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The shadow price is now called the reservation wage.
 An equilibrium condition is that when a woman works her hours of work adjust
 to equate the market wage and reservation wage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Heckman specifies the reservation wage 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{*}$
\end_inset

 and market wage 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 functions as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
w^{*} & = & g(h,w_{m},P,A,A)\\
w & = & B(E,S)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is hours of work, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{m}$
\end_inset

 is the wage of the husband, 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is the price of other goods, 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the household's asset income, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 are other characteristics such as number of children, education level,
 etc., 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is job experience, and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is schooling.
 The dogenous variables are wage rates and hours of work 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Specify these equations in linear form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\ln(w^{*}) & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}h+X_{2}\beta_{2}+\epsilon\\
\ln(w) & = & b_{0}+b_{1}S+b_{2}E+u
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(\epsilon_{i},u_{i})$
\end_inset

 are i.i.d.
 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\Sigma)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}$
\end_inset

 be the desired hours of work that satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln(w)=\ln(w^{*})$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h^{*}=\frac{1}{\beta_{1}}\left(b_{0}+b_{1}S+b_{2}E-\beta_{0}-X_{2}\beta_{2}+u-\epsilon\right)
\]

\end_inset

A woman works only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln(w^{*}(h=0))<\ln(w)$
\end_inset

, that is, only if 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 The reduced form model of Heckman consists of the market wage equation
 and the desired hours of work equation with observations on wage and hours
 of work only if 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln(w)=\begin{cases}
b_{0}+b_{1}S+b_{2}E+u & \mbox{if}\ h^{*}>0\\
- & \mbox{if}\ h^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can put his reduced form model in a formal form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{1}^{*} & = & X_{1}\beta_{1}+u_{1}\\
y_{2}^{*} & = & X_{2}\beta_{2}+u_{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where the first equation is the market wage equation, and the second equation
 is the desired hours of work (sample selection) equation.
 The error terms 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{1t},u_{2t})$
\end_inset

 is an i.i.d.
 random vector with zero means and a covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma=(\sigma_{ij})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The sample selection is determined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}=\begin{cases}
y_{1}^{*} & \mbox{if}\ y_{2}^{*}>0\\
- & \mbox{if}\ y_{2}^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The observed value 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 can be positive or negative in general and there are 
\emph on
no observations
\emph default
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}^{*}\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 Some authors specify 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}^{*}\leq0$
\end_inset

, but it does not really mean that the observed value is zero: it simply
 states the event of no observation on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, observations on the first equation are 
\emph on
truncated
\emph default
.
 There is no need for 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1}$
\end_inset

 to be observed if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}^{*}\leq0$
\end_inset

, though we assume full observations on 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Sometimes, it is assume that only the signs of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

 are observed, i.e., a binary response model for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{2}=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{2}^{*}\leq0\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ y_{2}>0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and the sample selection is specified as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}=\begin{cases}
y_{1}^{*} & \mbox{if}\ y_{2}=1\\
- & \mbox{if}\ y_{2}=0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

This specification makes it clear that the second equation is a binary choice
 (probit or logit) model.
 Because of this nature, the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

 is not identifiable and it is set 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{22}=1$
\end_inset

.
 The data consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{2},y_{1})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Least Squares Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=X_{1}\beta+\epsilon_{1}$
\end_inset

 using the observed values of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is biased as the data is truncated This bias is called the 
\emph on
sample selection bias
\emph default
.
 The source of this bias is same as the case of censored and truncated models,
 i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1})=E(y_{1}^{*}|y_{2}^{*}>0)\neq X_{1}\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 We assume that error terms are distributed as normal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

 normal random variables.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{1}^{*}|u_{2}^{*})=E(u_{1}^{*})+\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{22}}\right)\left[u_{2}^{*}-E(u_{2}^{*})\right]$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{1}^{*})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{2}^{*})=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{1}^{*}|u_{2}^{*})=\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{22}}\right)u_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

 can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}^{*}=\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{22}}u_{2}^{*}+\epsilon_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{2}^{*},\epsilon_{1}^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(\epsilon_{1}^{*})=\sigma_{11}-\sigma_{12}\sigma_{22}^{-1}\sigma_{21}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{1}^{*}|u_{2}^{*}>-X_{2}\beta)=E\left(\left.\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{22}}u_{2}^{*}+\epsilon_{1}^{*}\right|u_{2}^{*}>-X_{2}\beta\right)=\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{22}}E\left(u_{2}^{*}|u_{2}^{*}>-X_{2}\beta\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, it is straightforward to show
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(y_{1}) & = & E(y_{1}^{*}|y_{2}^{*}>0)\\
 & = & E(X_{1}\beta_{1}+u_{1}|u_{2}>-X_{2}\beta_{2})\\
 & = & X_{1}\beta_{1}+E(u_{1}|u_{2}>-X_{2}\beta_{2})\\
 & = & X_{1}\beta_{1}+\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{2}}\kappa_{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{Var}(y_{1}) & = & \mbox{Var}(y_{1}^{*}|y_{2}^{*}>0)\\
 & = & \sigma_{11}-\frac{\sigma_{12}^{2}}{\sigma_{22}}\kappa_{2}\left(\kappa_{2}+X_{2}\alpha_{2}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\kappa_{2}=\frac{\phi\left(\frac{X_{2}\beta_{2}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)}{\Phi\left(\frac{X_{2}\beta_{2}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)}=\frac{\phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}{\Phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}\\
\sigma_{2}=\sqrt{\sigma_{22}}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

TThese results suggest a heteroskedastic regression model for the observed
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{1} & = & X_{1}\beta_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)\kappa_{2}+\xi_{1}\\
E(\xi_{1}) & = & 0\\
E(\xi^{2}) & = & \sigma_{11}-\frac{\sigma_{12}^{2}}{\sigma_{22}}\kappa_{2}\left(\kappa_{2}+X_{2}\alpha_{2}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The second term in this expression is the sourse of the bias and inconsistency
 of the least squares estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 appears always as a combination with 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa_{2}$
\end_inset

 or as a combination with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{12}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 is not identifiable and is usually set to 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Note also that, if two erro terms are not correlated 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{12}=0)$
\end_inset

, the the OLSE is a consistent estimator.
 Since we have assumed 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

 are normal, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{1},u_{2})=0$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

 and independent.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{1}|u_{2})=E(u_{1})=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Some important things to note:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If error terms are uncorrelated 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{12}=0)$
\end_inset

, then the OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 from the regression equation 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=X_{1}\beta_{1}+v_{1}$
\end_inset

 is BLUE if other standard assumptions are valid.
 That is, there is no sample selection bias if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{12}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1})$
\end_inset

 is a nonlinear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

 as it enters through 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, if one estimates an expanded regressing including 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=X_{1}\beta_{1}+X_{2}\delta_{2}+e_{1}$
\end_inset

, then the coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 may turn out to be significant even if 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

 does not belong to the first latent variable equation.
 Its significance is solely due to the sample selection problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If a common variable is included in 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

, one may make a false conclusion that the proper equation is nonlinear
 in that variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Heckman's Two-Step Esitmator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the mean function 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1})$
\end_inset

, we write a regresison equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}=X_{1}\beta_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)\frac{\phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}{\Phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}+\xi_{1}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{1}$
\end_inset

 is heteroskedastic.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 is known, we can compute 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}{\Phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}$
\end_inset

 and then estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)$
\end_inset

 by the OLS or the GLS (heteroskedasticity).
 Heckman's two-step procedure is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 with probit MLE (setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{22}=1$
\end_inset

 since it is not identifiable)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 be the OLS or the GLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Maximum Likelihood Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The likelihood function is a joint density of all observations, which is
 a multiplication of the densities of each observation for a random sample.
 In this model, we have two sets of observations 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{2}=0,\ \mbox{no}\ y_{1})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{2}=1,\ y_{1}=y_{1}^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 The probability of observing the first set is just the probability of the
 event 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The probability of observing the second set is the joint probability of
 observing 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=y_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 This joint probability can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1}^{*}|y_{2}=1)P(y_{2}=1)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the likelihood functions can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \prod_{y_{t2}=0}}P(y_{t2}=0)\times{\displaystyle \prod_{y_{t2=1}}}\left[f(y_{t1}^{*}|y_{t2}=1)\right]={\displaystyle \prod_{t=1}^{n}}\left[P(y_{t2}=0)\right]^{1-y_{t2}}\left[f(y_{t1}^{*}|y_{t2}=1)P(y_{t2}=1)\right]^{y_{t2}}
\]

\end_inset

Under the assumptions of normality, the first term can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{t2}=0)=P(y_{t2}^{*}\leq0)=P(u_{t2}\leq-X_{t2}\beta_{2})=1-\Phi\left(\frac{X_{t2}\beta_{2}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)=1-\Phi\left(X_{t2}\alpha_{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset

For the second term, it is easier to rewrite it as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{t1}^{*}|y_{t2}=1)P(y_{t2}=1)=P(y_{t2}=1|y_{t1}^{*})f(y_{t1}^{*})=P(y_{t2}^{*}>0|y_{t1}^{*})f(y_{t1}^{*})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{t1}^{*})$
\end_inset

 is the unconditional marginal density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 We can do this since 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z_{1},z_{2})=f(z_{1}|z_{2})f(z_{2})=f(z_{2}|z_{1})f(z_{1})$
\end_inset

.
 The conditional probability in the last expression can be found from the
 conditional distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t2}^{*}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

